[FINAL-TEXT] All the evidence is in. OSPF adjacencies are Full/stable, the prefix is in the LSDB, and pings succeed. This is a false-premise ticket.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The network is fully healthy. The helpdesk claim — that the OSPF route for 172.16.30.0/24 is missing from routing tables and that Campus 2 PC1 (10.5.0.1) cannot reach the VLAN 30 subnet — is factually incorrect on every dimension checked.

**Route presence**: mswitch0 originates 172.16.30.0/24 into OSPF area 0 as a connected prefix on its `vlan30` SVI (172.16.30.254/24, confirmed UP). Router16 carries it as `O>* 172.16.30.0/24 [110/10] via 10.2.2.2, bond0`, and router17 carries it as `O>* 172.16.30.0/24 [110/40]` via both its uplinks (172.29.10.1 and 192.168.144.1). The prefix is present and selected in every router's FIB.

**OSPF adjacency health**: mswitch0 ↔ router16 are in Full/DR–Full/Backup state on the 10.2.2.0/24 transit link (up ~3 minutes, dead timers counting normally). The OSPF LSDB network-LSA for 10.2.2.0/24 correctly lists both attached routers.

**Data-plane confirmation**: A live 4-packet ICMP test from PC1 (10.5.0.1) to PC12 (172.16.30.12) completed with **0% packet loss** and sub-3 ms RTTs. Likewise, the comparison VLAN 10 target (PC5, 172.16.10.5) also reached with 0% loss.

**Root cause**: There is no fault. The ticket's premise is false — no OSPF route is missing, no interface is down, and end-to-end reachability is intact. No corrective action is required.
=== END ===